<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constitution of Japan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as constitution of 1947</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> was adopted in 1947 under the Allied occupation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, replacing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889. It made the emperor a symbol of the state with no political power and established popular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. It also renounced war as a sovereign right, marking the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constitution-of-japan.jpg" alt="Constitution of Japan" width="292" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Japanese government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nihon_Kenpo01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
